comments:
@deanm0000 I understand. I'm surprised how poor the terminology for these objects are. AFAICT America/New_York is a "timezone identifier". For EST/EDT I've found the terms "timezone record" or "timezone abbreviation" (as you mention).

FWIW, my ticket is about genuinely mixed columns of different timezone identifiers.  Clarifying the docs about these things would be beneficial for those of us finding themselves halfway through the timezone rabbit hole.  The world would possibly have been a better place if a 64-bit type had been defined that reserved 127 bits for a timezone, and we wouldn't have to coerce a column to a single one (this remains a special case everywhere I look).

What's more, is that it might be an improvement if columns were prevented from being associated with a timezone abbreviation, that is, restricted to a timezone identifier.  Pandas allows timezone identifiers, abbreviations, and other zones like "LMT" or even "FixedOffset". IMHO it would be improved if it failed instead of allowing those, requiring instead the user to provide a timezone identifier where there's none.  (But then someone would have to take the users for a visit down the rabbit hole...)
